    "id": "art_minor-caged-one-window-two-names",
    "slug": "minor-caged-one-window-two-names",
    "title": "One Window, Two Names",
    "summary": "A minor's five windows sit three frets lower than C major's same-lettered ones, and exactly two spans — frets 4–8 and 9–13 — belong to both maps at once.",
    "tags": ["caged", "minor"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "If the notes are identical, surely the boxes are too. They aren't. Three of the five sit somewhere else entirely, and the two that do coincide are worth naming precisely, because they're the bridge between everything you already know and everything this pathway is about to teach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Here's why. A window is a fret span positioned by the chord's " },
        { "text": "root", "marks": ["bold"] },
        { "text": ", the same anchoring rule the " },
        { "text": "root ladder", "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "caged-root-ladder" } },
        { "text": " used, and " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " sits three semitones below " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". So every A minor window sits three frets lower on the neck than the C major window with the same letter — nothing about the neck itself changed, only where each letter's window starts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The two ladders, form by form. One notation note first: " },
        { "text": "5·3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " means string 5, fret 3 — string 1 is the high " },
        { "text": "e", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": [[{ "text": "Form" }], [{ "text": "C major" }], [{ "text": "A minor" }]],
      "rows": [
        [
          [{ "text": "C" }],
          [{ "text": "0–4", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "9–13", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "A" }],
          [{ "text": "2–6", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "0–3", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "G" }],
          [{ "text": "4–8", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "1–5", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "E" }],
          [{ "text": "7–11", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "4–8", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "D" }],
          [{ "text": "9–13", "marks": ["code"] }],
          [{ "text": "6–10", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Nine of those ten spans are five frets wide. The odd one out is A minor's A form: it would start a fret behind the nut, so it runs "
        },
        { "text": "0–3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " with one fret of it missing. Same form, less room — and this chapter does not need it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Same neck, same dots underneath — two different sets of bands. Compare the edges, not the roots: it's where each band starts and ends that tells the real story here."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": { "root": "C" }
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "caged-ladder",
      "props": { "root": "A", "quality": "minor" }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [{ "text": "Exactly two spans coincide, checked across all twenty-five pairs:" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        [
          { "text": "Frets " },
          { "text": "4–8", "marks": ["code"] },
          { "text": " are C major's " },
          { "text": "G form", "marks": ["bold"] },
          { "text": " and A minor's " },
          { "text": "E form", "marks": ["bold"] },
          { "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          { "text": "Frets " },
          { "text": "9–13", "marks": ["code"] },
          { "text": " are C major's " },
          { "text": "D form", "marks": ["bold"] },
          { "text": " and A minor's " },
          { "text": "C form", "marks": ["bold"] },
          { "text": "." }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Nothing else lines up, and it's worth seeing why rather than just taking the two coincidences on faith. Shift the whole C major ladder down three frets, and each form lands on the span of whichever form sits three frets above it in C. Reading C major's window starts — "
        },
        { "text": "0, 2, 4, 7, 9", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", then " },
        { "text": "12", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the only gaps of three are " },
        { "text": "G→E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "D→C", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". So exactly two forms find a partner; the other three land in gaps of two, where nothing is waiting for them."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Take the " },
        { "text": "4–8", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " window and read it twice. Seventeen positions, identical dots, two different sets of labels:"
        }
