    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " at " },
        { "text": "6·8", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " was C major's " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "; here it's A minor's " },
        { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". The " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " at " },
        { "text": "6·5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " was the " },
        { "text": "6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "; here it's the " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Every one of the seventeen positions works the same way — same fret, same finger, different name — because the label was never a property of the dot. It's a property of which chord you're calling home."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "tip",
      "spans": [{ "text": "The dots do not move; the labels do." }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One paragraph on a shape inside that same window you may already know. On frets "
        },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "8", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — twelve dots — sits the five-note version almost every self-taught guitarist already plays, the one they call \"the A minor pentatonic at fret 5\". It's the same dots as CAGED's G form of C major, note for note, fret for fret. Two things you may have known separately for years — a pentatonic box and a CAGED form — turn out to be one thing wearing two names. That's as far as this chapter goes with it — the pentatonic itself, and what its five notes actually give you, waits for its own chapter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "You don't have to take any of this on trust. Check it yourself in the " },
        { "text": "Scale Visualizer", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/scale-visualizer" } },
        { "text": ": set the root to " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " and the scale to natural minor, leave the position system on CAGED, and page through — the pager labels frets "
        },
        { "text": "4–8", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " \"E form\". Switch the root to " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " and the scale to major and page again; the same span is now labelled \"G form\". The app agrees with this lesson by construction."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Five windows, carved differently from C major's five but built from the exact same notes, and five A minor chord shapes waiting inside them. The only thing that has to move to find any of them is the third. That's Chapter 2."
        }
      ]
    }
